01:40:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It makes perfect sense that you would think that! Our brains are literally wired to trust our senses, and your senses are telling you, loud and clear, that the tile is cold and the couch is warm. But here is the trick: **your hand is a liar.** Or, more accurately, your hand is a *heat-flow meter*, not a *thermometer*. Science doesn't deny what you are feeling. You *are* feeling a very real difference when you touch those objects! It’s just that you aren't feeling their **temperature**. You are feeling **your own body heat draining away.** To understand why your sense of touch tricks you, think about this famous real-world example: **The Swimming Pool Effect.** Imagine it’s a perfectly comfortable 70°F (21°C) day. You are standing outside in your swimsuit, and the air feels great. Then, you jump into a swimming pool where the water is *also exactly 70°F*. The water feels freezing! How can the water feel freezing when it is the exact same temperature as the air? It’s because water is a much better conductor than air. The water sucks the 98.6°F heat out of your body about 25 times faster than the air does. Your brain doesn't know the temperature of the water; it only knows that *you are losing heat rapidly*, so it sends a "COLD!" alarm. **The exact same thing is happening in your living room:** * The **metal lamp** and **floor tile** are like the pool water. They are dense and highly conductive. When your warm hand touches them, they instantly suck the heat out of your skin. Your brain yells, "Cold!" * The **couch** is like the air. Fabric and foam are insulators (they are full of tiny air pockets). When you touch the couch, it doesn't absorb your body heat. Your heat stays in your hand, so your brain says, "Warm." **How to prove it to yourself (The Ice Cube Experiment):** You don't just have to take science's word for it. You can prove it in your kitchen right now with two ice cubes. 1. Take a heavy metal frying pan (which feels "cold" to t